Pakistan and the U.S. were allies during the Afghanistan War/War on Terror, during which the U.S. provided [significant funding to Pakistan](https://tribune.com.pk/story/1498815/war-terror-aid-pakistan-received-33-4bn-us) primarily to counter terrorism. However, following the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan, Pakistan lost much of its strategic importance to Washington.&#x20;

The ongoing war between the U.S. and Iran has presented Pakistan with an opportunity for mediation. Pakistan acted as a mediator between both sides, which led to the draft of [the Islamabad MoU](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamabad_Memorandum). Although it was ultimately unsuccessful, it still managed to establish a 60-day ceasefire extension to negotiate a final peace deal. President Trump has on numerous occasions [praised Pakistan's leadership](https://tribune.com.pk/story/2630836/trump-praises-pm-shehbaz-as-terrific-man-recalls-india-pakistan-mediation-at-unga) with great fervor, particularly its prime minister and Army Chief.

Following this, in July 2026, Pakistan officials sought [a \$10 billion exchange stabilisation facility](https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/pakistan-seeks-10-billion-us-backstop-facility-boost-reserves-source-says-2026-07-21/), an arrangement to boost the South Asian country's reserve, ease the pressure on the rupee and reduce its reliance on multilateral financing. On September 17, Pakistan's Finance Minister Muhammad Aurangzeb said that he expects [a U.S. response within the next two months](https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/pakistan-eyes-bigger-china-swap-line-expects-us-financing-decision-soon-2026-09-17/).
